            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Use stars and bars to distribute ten occurrences among six prime factors.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $a,b,c,d,e,f$ count the occurrences of $2,3,5,7,11,13$, respectively. The product is $2^a3^b5^c7^d11^e13^f$, where\n$$a+b+c+d+e+f=10.$$\nUnique prime factorisation means different non-negative exponent lists give different products; roll order does not matter. Represent the ten occurrences by ten stars and separate the six prime groups with five bars. Choose the five bar positions among fifteen positions:\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=\\binom{10+6-1}{6-1}\\\\N&=\\binom{15}{5}\\\\N&=3003.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
